{
 "name": "bridge_ring_pairing_divergence",
 "face_ring_start": "compared",
 "description": "Bridging two disjoint quads: how the two rings are paired, which way the new quads are wound, and what a segment count does. Three cells, all on the same pair of islands, all with counts agreeing on both sides except the third. (a) Plain bridge: identical vertices AND identical rings -- and all 4 new quads are wound the OTHER WAY from the reference's. Only a winding-sensitive comparison sees this; a winding-blind one reports parity. (b) The same bridge with the flip flag on: the reference's flip REVERSES THE WINDING of the same four quads, ours RE-PAIRS the two rings one step round, which is a different set of quads -- e.g. ours joins the edge (0,0,0)-(1,0,0) of one island to (0,0,3)-(0,0,2) of the other where the reference joins it to (1,0,2)-(0,0,2). (c) Three segments across the gap: the reference lays 12 quads on two intermediate rings at 1/3 and 2/3; our bridge has no segment count at all, so it lays the same single span as (a) -- 8 vertices and 4 faces against 16 and 12. That third cell is a CAPABILITY GAP: closing it means adding a segment count, not correcting an arithmetic.",
 "source": "Reference side: headless command-port capture of the bridge tool with segments 1, with the flip flag, and with segments 3 (dirty-geometry sweep, task 1123, cases bridge_faces_islands, bridge_faces_flip_islands, bridge_seg3_islands). vibe3d side: live drive of this build.",
 "provenance": {
  "schema": 1,
  "source": "live-capture",
  "reference": "ref-editor@11.2v3",
  "method": "command",
  "captured_utc": "2026-08-17",
  "harness": "tools/local/capture/dirty (dirty-geometry x tool sweep, task 1123)",
  "task": "1140",
  "notes": "INSTRUMENT: the reference side is a BEHAVIOURAL CAPTURE driven through the reference editor's own headless command port -- no display, no pointer, no debugger and no static read of its binary; every number in `reference` is a coordinate the reference itself printed after running the operation on the mesh in `input`. The vibe3d side is a LIVE DRIVE of this build over the HTTP test API through the very steps this fixture stores, read back from /api/model. Both engines are given the SAME base mesh (the reference's own dump, injected verbatim) and the selection travels by COORDINATE, so no element index is assumed to mean the same thing on both sides. Nothing here is an inference about the reference's internals; where a MECHANISM is guessed at, the case note says so in that word."
 },
